Dirty Pipe is a Linux kernel vulnerability that was discovered in 2016. It affects the way the kernel handles copy-on-write (COW) memory. When a process writes to a COW memory region, the kernel creates a new copy of the memory and updates the process's page tables to point to the new copy. However, if the process doesn't actually write to the memory, the kernel doesn't create a new copy. Instead, it modifies the existing copy in place.

This behavior can be exploited to gain kernel privileges. By carefully crafting a COW memory region, an attacker can cause the kernel to modify its own memory without writing to it. This allows the attacker to execute arbitrary code in the kernel.

